  rows — which at 2-20 users is nothing.

  [1mB. Direct-write notifications, no event log.[0m One table. An action computes recipients and inserts notification rows
  carrying their own description text. Simplest thing that works.

  [3mCost:[0m no audit trail, so task history becomes a second system later. Notification text is frozen at write time, so a
  task renamed afterward shows a stale title in old notifications. Re-deriving recipients after a subscription bug is
  impossible — the rows are all you have.

  [1mC. Event log only, fan-out on read.[0m One table. Write events; compute each user's feed at read time by joining events
  against subscription rules. No per-recipient rows at all.

  [3mCost:[0m it seems simpler and isn't. Read state needs its own per-user-per-event table, which is the fan-out table you
  were avoiding, minus the indexability. Unread counts become a join over the whole event history. Its one genuine
  advantage — subscription changes retroactively reshape your feed — is a property most users find confusing rather than
  useful.

  Cheap to change later: event [3mtypes[0m, notification copy, the feed UI. Expensive: whether a per-recipient row exists,
  since read state, unread counts, and email delivery all hang off it.

